  "response": "Michael Doyle's 1983 essays identified a separate peace holding among liberal states. The normative explanation holds that democracies externalize domestic norms of peaceful dispute resolution to relations with other democracies, whereas the institutional explanation holds that elected leaders face electoral accountability for the costs of war. At the level of observable behavior, at least one of two things is the case, and perhaps both: democratic leaders are removed from office for initiating costly wars, and democratic publics hold normative objections to the use of force against fellow democracies. Some years after Doyle's essays appeared, Zeev Maoz and Bruce Russett tested normative and structural models using militarized interstate dispute data for 1946 to 1986. The Polity index codes regime type on a scale running from 0 to 100. That test led them to report that the normative model outperformed the structural model in their statistical tests; by contrast, the institutional explanation had rested on the answerability of elected leaders at the polls for the costs of war.\n\nJames Fearon's audience-cost argument holds that democratic leaders suffer domestic punishment for backing down in a crisis. Precisely because that punishment is anticipated, Kenneth Schultz argued that the presence of an opposition party makes democratic signals in crises more credible. Schultz also found that democratic institutions make coercive signals less credible than those of autocracies, a result that cannot be squared with the credibility advantage he attributes to competitive opposition.\n\nSebastian Rosato published a critique of the normative and institutional logics of the democratic peace in the American Political Science Review in 2003. The survey-experimental evidence that subsequently reoriented the debate toward individual-level attitudes arrived only after that critique had appeared. Michael Tomz and Jessica Weeks ran survey experiments in which respondents were less supportive of military strikes against democracies, and that reluctance confirms John Owen's argument that liberal perceptions of which states count as democracies drive the peace.\n\nThe upshot is that the two accounts are no longer treated as rivals to be settled by a single test. Dyadic dispute data remain compatible with both logics once measurement choices are varied, so the weight of adjudication has shifted to designs that interrogate the micro-foundations each account presupposes, and to the question of how actors identify the regimes toward which restraint is owed.\n\nAdjudicating between the normative and the institutional readings of the democratic peace is a problem of research design rather than of interpretation alone, since both accounts predict much the same dyadic regularity. Scholars therefore search for implications on which the two diverge: whether restraint tracks the character of the adversary's regime or the costs a leader expects to bear at home, whether the pattern survives alternative measurements of regime type, and whether the mechanism each account requires can be observed directly in public attitudes and in crisis signaling.",
